    {
      "id": "7f98fbecee2062b10b8b28e1959f386672a475d7",
      "tag": "autogenerated:ai-review",
      "author": {
        "_account_id": 1055,
        "name": "Marc Vef",
        "email": "mvef@whamcloud.com",
        "username": "mvef"
      },
      "date": "2026-10-05 15:25:39.000000000",
      "message": "Patch Set 2:\n\n(13 comments)\n\n**[Marc Bot - AI review - opus]**\n\nNeeds a refresh: several field descriptions and all three examples contradict what liblustreapi actually does. I checked the examples by running them against the library on a local tree; details are in the (defect) comments.\n\nWhile it's being refreshed: about a third of the page (Internal Traversal Implementation, Work Queue Architecture, Queue Operations, and the path-buffer and fd notes) documents private internals that callers can't use, such as llapi_semantic_traverse(), work_unit_create_and_add() and the fwq_* fields. That text has already drifted from the code. On error the worker doesn't broadcast; parallel_find() does that after draining the queue. And the queue is a single shared FIFO, not work-stealing. Consider replacing it with the contract callers can rely on: with fp_thread_count > 1, callbacks run concurrently on up to that many threads. Each queued directory gets its own copy of find_param, so a callback's writes to param fields don't carry across directories, while the fp_cb_data pointer is shared. Visit order is not depth-first.\n\n(review iteration 1 of this change, with accumulated review memory)",
      "accounts_in_message": [],
      "_revision_number": 2
    }
  ],
